# Loom — line endings and what counts as text.
#
# Loom is developed on Linux and Windows, on the same machine, dual-booted.
# Without this file every checkout's line endings depend on that machine's
# core.autocrlf setting, so one clone configured differently shows the whole
# tree as modified on the other OS. Here the rule travels with the repository
# instead of living in a config I have to remember to set.
#
# text=auto: git decides text-vs-binary by content, and stores every text file
# with LF. The working tree gets whatever the platform wants (CRLF on Windows
# under autocrlf, LF on Linux), which is why there is no eol= here - forcing
# eol=lf would rewrite the entire Windows working tree for no gain.
* text=auto

# The image formats, stated rather than sniffed. Git's heuristic already gets
# these right, but a template is a reference image that digit recognition
# matches pixel-for-pixel: if one were ever treated as text and had its bytes
# "converted", the failure would be a silent misread, not an error. That is
# the worst failure mode this project has, so the rule is written down.
*.png  binary
*.webp binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary

# Build orders are the user's data in someone else's format (RTS Overlay).
# Marking them text is what keeps a build authored on Windows from arriving
# on Linux with CRLF baked into the committed file.
*.json text
*.md   text
*.py   text
*.txt  text
*.yml  text
*.yaml text
